<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SAND_SWIMMERS_MOD_1 — Deep Sand: an impassable sand terrain that behaves like deep
  water for pathing and fishing, without being water at all.

  MECHANISM, verified against RimWorld 1.6 + Odyssey source (not guessed):
  - `TerrainDef.IsWater` (Verse/TerrainDef.cs) is `HasTag("Water")` — a TAG check, not
    a hardcoded terrain-name list. Any TerrainDef carrying the "Water" tag reads as
    water to every system that calls `.IsWater` (pathing, `Zone_Fishing.IsFishable`,
    `SwimPathFinder`, `JoyGiver_GoSwimming`, `WaterSource`, flood/explosion FX, etc.).
  - `Zone_Fishing.IsFishable(cell)` (RimWorld/Zone_Fishing.cs) requires
    `cell.GetTerrain(map).IsWater` AND `cell.GetWaterBodyType(map) == FishType`, where
    `FishType` comes from the zone's `TerrainDef.waterBodyType` (Freshwater/Saltwater —
    a closed two-value field, `Verse/WaterBodyType.cs`; there is no "Sand" water body
    type, and adding one would need C#). Declaring `waterBodyType: Freshwater` here is
    what lets a fishing zone actually form fishable cells on Deep Sand.
  - Passability Impassable is what blocks ordinary pawn pathing — the same mechanism
    `WaterDeep` (`Defs/Core/TerrainDefs/Terrain_Water.xml`) uses for "you cannot walk
    across this." This def is written from scratch rather than ParentName="WaterBase"
    to avoid inheriting water-only baggage (the `SoakingWet` traversedThought, the
    `Map/WaterDepth` shader, `floodTerrain`) that reads wrong on sand.

  Engine/content split (NAMING_SCHEME_PLAN.md §1): this terrain is a pure mechanic —
  "impassable ground you can fish in" — with zero Star Wars flavor, so it is RM tier
  and lives here in ManyWaters (already the RM-tier "gathers all water effects/types"
  engine, MANYWATERS_GENERIC_SPLIT_1) rather than a new mod. It names no biome of its
  own, matching that mod's existing generic/biome-agnostic pattern. The Star-Wars-
  flavored fish family that gets caught here is RSW tier (SWBestiary); the Ash'karr
  biome hookup (which BiomeDef actually offers this fish list) is RUT tier
  (UtinniPatches) — see Patches/SandFishing_CrackedLands.xml there.

  SITING — open question, NOT resolved here (world-tile placement is out of scope for
  this item; see the item file). `dune_sea.md` §6 hard-bans "medium-sized fauna" in
  the dune sea proper (giant-or-grain-scale only) and `ExtremeDesert` is literally the
  SAME BiomeDef for both the dune sea (θ0-40) and deep desert (θ50-69, Glare/Kiln) —
  there is no BiomeDef-level way to scope a fish family to deep-desert tiles only
  without leaking into dune-sea tiles. The RUT hookup therefore targets Cracked Lands
  (`ZBiome_Badlands`) only, which carries no such ban. Wiring ExtremeDesert/Desert too
  (the item's other two siting suggestions) is a follow-up that first needs an answer
  to that conflict — either keep any ExtremeDesert Deep Sand pool at θ≥40 (deep desert
  proper), or give the fish family a giant/grain-scale variant fit for the dune sea.

  ART — PLACEHOLDER. texturePath below points at the vanilla `Terrain/Surfaces/SoftSand`
  texture as a deliberate stand-in so this never renders magenta; a real "sunken,
  rippled sand pool, visibly deeper/darker than surrounding sand" texture is OWED
  (generating-rimworld-sprites skill) and is not part of this pass.

  ANTI-EXPONENTIAL: this def carries no yield lever of its own (that lives in the
  biome's fishTypes/maxFishPopulation, RUT side) — it only makes cells fishable, it
  does not make fish more common.
-->
<Defs>

  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_DeepSand</defName>
    <label>deep sand</label>
    <description>A pool of sand deep enough to swallow a person whole. The surface looks like any other dune, but there is nothing solid beneath it — step out onto it and you sink, slowly and completely. Nothing walks across deep sand. Some things live in it.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/SoftSand</texturePath>
    <!-- PLACEHOLDER ART — see header. -->
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <categoryType>Sand</categoryType>
    <renderPrecedence>353</renderPrecedence>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <natural>true</natural>
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <pathCost>300</pathCost>
    <avoidWander>true</avoidWander>
    <canFreeze>false</canFreeze>
    <changeable>false</changeable>
    <holdSnowOrSand>false</holdSnowOrSand>
    <extinguishesFire>true</extinguishesFire>
    <extraDeteriorationFactor>3</extraDeteriorationFactor>
    <extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>180</extraNonDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>18</extraDraftedPerceivedPathCost>
    <waterBodyType>Freshwater</waterBodyType>
    <!-- Not literal water — Freshwater is the only bucket BiomeFishTypes offers that
         isn't briny; picked so the biome-side fishTypes patch (RUT) has somewhere to
         hang its freshwater_Common/Uncommon catches. -->
    <filthAcceptanceMask>
      <li>None</li>
    </filthAcceptanceMask>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <!-- The load-bearing tag: TerrainDef.IsWater == HasTag("Water"). Everything
           else in this def follows from being tagged Water — impassability reads
           the same as deep water, and Zone_Fishing/WorkGiver_Fish/JobDriver_Fish
           work unmodified, no Harmony patch needed. -->
    </tags>
    <tools>
      <li>
        <label>sand</label>
        <capacities>
          <li>KickMaterialInEyes</li>
        </capacities>
        <hediff>SandInEyes</hediff>
        <!-- Reuses vanilla Sand/SoftSand's own hazard hediff rather than WaterInEyes. -->
        <cooldownTime>1.5</cooldownTime>
      </li>
    </tools>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-5</Beauty>
      <BeautyOutdoors>-3</BeautyOutdoors>
    </statBases>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
